A||N||3||Mr. Farknocker||5=spd Getrag Selector Cable Bind||||5||
Z||000000||Mr. Farknocker||12-24-2001||04:48 PM||||My 5-spd selector cable on my '87 GT (Getrag)is binding and requires <BR>great effort to move.  Its the one that moves when you move the <BR>stick left and right. The cable was replaced when I bought the car <BR>and has seen about 20K of use. I removed the cable from the car <BR>and the cable looks ok except for a few scrapes and scratches on <BR>the outer plastic casing. I tried to spray lubricant through the <BR>cable but it is nearly impossible to get it down the line. Can this <BR>be fixed or do I have to buy a new one?<P>Rich<BR>'87 GT<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-24-2001||06:02 PM||||Since you have it removed from the car, can you move the inner cable in and out freely (with little effort)?  If you can then there is something binding elsewhere or you have the cable misrouted, although I doubt that a misrouted cable would casue these problems.  <P>It sounds like the cable is starting to seize up and needs to be replaced.  You can drill a small hole into the casing to spray some WD-40 inside to lubricate it.  You might try that before buying another one.<P>If you do need a new one I believe Rodney Dickman sells them pretty reasonably.  His website is:  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P><BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||12-25-2001||02:42 PM||||Your post brought the following article to mind, courtesy the "Minnesota Fieros Forever" club: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=0jx6ce8q&Catid=21&Id=27&Action=Edit" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=0jx6ce8q&Catid=21&Id=27&Action=Edit</A> <P>Don't know whether or not it applies to your situation, but hope it helps   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||Mr. Farknocker||12-26-2001||03:36 AM||||Thanks for the article.  The symptoms described in the article sounds like the problems I've been experiencing.  I will try to lube the cable before I plunk down 70 clams for a new one.  One other owner suggested that that I clamp one end of a  heater hose to one end of the cable and that I fill the hose with transmission oil and pressurize the same using a compressor to force the oil through the cable casing.<P>I'll let you guys know how this turns out.<P>Rich.<BR>'87 GT||||reg||1
